  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the eastern Great Lakes provides a tranquil Labor \nDay. High clouds are spilling in from convection elsewhere in the \nMidwest, however. This activity is on the decline, so, generally \nmore sun than clouds today. Our pesky upper-level ridge, now \ncentered over western MN, will exert a hint of influence overhead \ntoday as temperatures creep up a touch warmer than Sunday; highs \ngenerally in the low-80s. The ridge tracks east Tuesday and \nWednesday but flattens through time as low pressure deepens over \nManitoba. A trailing cold front moves through Wednesday bringing a \n40-60% chance of showers and thunderstorms. Forecast soundings ahead \nof the front Wednesday afternoon show marginal support for isoalted \nsevere thunderstorms. Lapse rates are poor, while instability and \nshear are both acceptable for early Autumn. Stray showers may linger \ninto Thursday morning due to the latitudinal orientation of the \noccluding front atop Southeast US high pressure. \n\nWhile Wednesday looks to be the warmest day of the week, cloud cover \npermitting (upper-80s), high pressure over the upper Midwest \nThursday ushers in upper-70s once again. In the progressive upper-\nair pattern the follows, additional chances for rain and storms \narrive next weekend.",
